# Graphs

A graph is a non-linear data structure consisting of a set of vertices (nodes) and edges (connections) between them. Unlike trees, graphs do not require a root node and can contain cycles.

## Basic Terminology

### Vertex (Node)

A vertex is an individual element in a graph.

Example:

```text
A, B, C, D
```

### Edge

An edge represents a connection between two vertices.

```text
A ----- B
```

Edge: `(A, B)`

---

## Example Graph

```text
A ----- B
|       |
|       |
C ----- D
```

### Vertices

```text
A, B, C, D
```

### Edges

```text
(A,B)
(B,D)
(D,C)
(C,A)
```

---

## Types of Graphs

### 1. Undirected Graph

```text
A ----- B
```

A connection exists in both directions.

```text
A ↔ B
```

---

### 2. Directed Graph (Digraph)

```text
A -----> B
```

The connection exists only in one direction.

```text
A → B
```

---

### 3. Weighted Graph

A graph where edges have weights or costs.

```text
A --5-- B
 \     /
  3   2
   \ /
    C
```

Weights:

```text
A-B = 5
A-C = 3
B-C = 2
```

---

## Degree of a Vertex

The degree of a vertex is the number of edges connected to it.

Example:

```text
A ----- B
|
|
C
```

Degree of A = 2

---

## Cycle

A cycle occurs when a path starts and ends at the same vertex.

```text
A ----- B
|       |
|       |
C ----- D
```

Cycle:

```text
A → B → D → C → A
```

---

# Graph Representation

Graphs can be represented in memory using two common methods:

## 1. Adjacency Matrix

An adjacency matrix is a 2D array where:

* 1 indicates a connection exists.
* 0 indicates no connection.

### Example Graph

```text
A ----- B
|
|
C
```

### Adjacency Matrix

```text
      A B C
    --------
A |   0 1 1
B |   1 0 0
C |   1 0 0
```

### Advantages

* Easy to check whether two vertices are connected.
* Simple implementation.

### Disadvantages

* Requires more memory.
* Inefficient for sparse graphs.

---

## 2. Adjacency List

An adjacency list stores the neighbors of each vertex.

### Example Graph

```text
A ----- B
|
|
C
```

### Adjacency List

```text
A → B → C

B → A

C → A
```

### Advantages

* Memory efficient.
* Ideal for sparse graphs.

### Disadvantages

* Checking whether two vertices are connected may take more time.

---

## Comparison

| Feature       | Adjacency Matrix | Adjacency List |
| ------------- | ---------------- | -------------- |
| Storage       | 2D Array         | List           |
| Memory Usage  | High             | Low            |
| Edge Lookup   | Fast             | Slower         |
| Sparse Graphs | Inefficient      | Efficient      |

---

